const CSA = {"version": "1.0", "html": "<p class=\"c\"><b>CLIENT SERVICE AGREEMENT</b></p>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>FOR THE PROVISION AND USE OF THE DIGITIZED INVOICE SYSTEM</b></p>\n<p class=\"j\">This Client Service Agreement (the “Agreement”) is made on the ____ day of ______________ 20____, at ____________________, Philippines, by and between:</p>\n<p class=\"j\">{{name}}, a {{type_lc}} duly organized and existing under the laws of the Republic of the Philippines (for a sole proprietor, a Filipino citizen of legal age), with Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN) {{tin}} and principal office at {{address}}, represented herein by {{signatory}}, in the capacity of {{position}}, hereinafter referred to as the “CLIENT”;</p>\n<p class=\"c\">and</p>\n<p class=\"j\">EASTLAND PRINTINK, INC., a corporation duly organized and existing under the laws of the Republic of the Philippines, with principal office at 92 R. Jacinto St., Canumay West, Valenzuela City 1443, represented herein by ______________________________, in the capacity of ______________________________, hereinafter referred to as “EPI.”</p>\n<p class=\"j\">The CLIENT and EPI are together called the “PARTIES.”</p>\n<h2>Background</h2>\n<p class=\"j\">A.  The CLIENT is a taxpayer that issues or will issue electronic invoices, and requires a system to generate, issue, preserve and verify them online and to send them to the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).</p>\n<p class=\"j\">B.  EPI provides the Digitized Invoice System (the “System”) as an electronic service provider for that purpose.</p>\n<p class=\"j\">C.  The PARTIES wish to set out the terms of that service, including a per-transaction fee based on the CLIENT’s plan.</p>\n<p class=\"j\">The PARTIES therefore agree as follows:</p>\n<h2>1.  DEFINITIONS</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">1.1</span><span class=\"t\">“BIR EIS” means the Electronic Invoicing System of the BIR.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">1.2</span><span class=\"t\">“E-Invoice” means an electronic invoice generated by the System for the CLIENT.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">1.3</span><span class=\"t\">“Billable Document” means an invoice that the System has validated, numbered and issued. These are not Billable Documents: a document the System rejects; a document voided because of an error of the System; and any retransmission of a document to the BIR EIS or to a buyer.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">1.4</span><span class=\"t\">“CLIENT Data” means all data entered into or produced by the System for the CLIENT, including E-Invoices, buyer details, sales data, acknowledgments, rejection records and audit logs.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">1.5</span><span class=\"t\">“Client Details”, “Service Level Schedule” and “Data Schedule” mean Schedules A, B and C to this Agreement.</span></p>\n<h2>2.  THE SERVICE</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.1</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall give the CLIENT access to the System to generate, issue, preserve and verify E-Invoices online, to send them to the BIR EIS, to deliver copies to the CLIENT’s buyers, and to prepare the CLIENT’s electronic sales reports.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.2</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall transmit E-Invoices and sales data to the BIR EIS in real time or near real time, and in every case within three (3) calendar days from the date of the transaction. When transmission is not possible, EPI shall securely queue and preserve the data, and shall reconcile and retransmit it within three (3) calendar days after the connection is restored, without loss, alteration, corruption or duplication.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.3</span><span class=\"t\">When the System itself is unavailable, EPI shall accept and queue the CLIENT’s sales where it can. On restoration the System shall issue the queued documents, in order, and deliver copies to buyers without the CLIENT having to resend them. Invoices the CLIENT issued manually in the meantime shall be recorded in the System and linked to their electronic copies.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.4</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall provide the service as an electronic service provider accredited by the BIR, and shall keep that accreditation in force. The accreditation reference is stated in the Client Details. If the accreditation is suspended, restricted or revoked, EPI shall tell the CLIENT in writing within two (2) business days and shall follow the directions of the BIR. Section 11.6 then applies.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.5</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall activate the CLIENT’s account, and live E-Invoices may be issued, only when: (a) the BIR has granted EPI’s accreditation; (b) EPI has received and verified the signed and notarized Agreement; (c) EPI has validated the CLIENT’s legal existence and BIR registration and its signatory’s authority, from the documents in Annex 2; (d) the CLIENT’s PTI Electronic Invoice registration is in the CLIENT’s own name; (e) the connection tests the BIR requires have been completed; and (f) EPI has confirmed the CLIENT’s plan under Section 4.2 and has received the Safety Deposit under Section 4.8.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.6</span><span class=\"t\">EPI grants the CLIENT a limited, non-exclusive, non-transferable right to use the System for its own invoicing while this Agreement is in force. EPI keeps all rights in the System. The CLIENT shall not copy, reverse engineer, resell or share access to the System, share its sign-in credentials, test the security of the System without EPI’s written consent, or use the System to issue false or fictitious invoices.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">2.7</span><span class=\"t\">If EPI reasonably finds that the CLIENT’s account is being used for fraud, or threatens the security of the System, EPI may stop new issuing on that account after telling the CLIENT, and shall restore it when the cause is cured. EPI shall not restrict the CLIENT’s access to its records.</span></p>\n<h2>3.  AUTHORIZATION TO EPI</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">3.1</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT authorizes EPI, in writing, to act on its behalf to:</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(a)</span><span class=\"t\">facilitate the application for, issuance of, or updating of the CLIENT’s PTI Electronic Invoice through the procedure the BIR prescribes;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(b)</span><span class=\"t\">manage the API integrations, certifications and pre-production testing that the BIR requires;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(c)</span><span class=\"t\">transmit the CLIENT’s E-Invoices and sales data to the BIR EIS; and</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(d)</span><span class=\"t\">arrange the notarization of this Agreement, by booking the notary, delivering the documents and collecting the notarized copies.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">3.2</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT remains the holder of its PTI Electronic Invoice registration and of every authority the BIR has issued to it. EPI acquires no proprietary interest in, or control over, any of them.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">3.3</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall not sign this Agreement or any other document for the CLIENT. The CLIENT’s authorized signatory shall personally sign and shall appear before the notary.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">3.4</span><span class=\"t\">This authorization lasts while the Agreement is in force. The CLIENT may withdraw it by written notice. Withdrawal does not affect EPI’s duties under Article 9. The CLIENT shall also sign the Authorization form in Annex 1.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">3.5</span><span class=\"t\">EPI pays the notarial fee for this Agreement. Each PARTY bears its own other costs of signing, such as travel and identification.</span></p>\n<h2>4.  SERVICE FEES</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.1</span><span class=\"t\">The PARTIES adopt a per-transaction fee set by the CLIENT’s plan. Each plan has a number of Billable Documents a month that it covers (the “Monthly Allowance”). Billable Documents above the Monthly Allowance and a margin of one percent (1%) in a calendar month are charged the excess fee of the plan:</span></p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Monthly Allowance</th><th>Allowance with 1% margin</th><th>Fee per Billable Document</th><th>Excess Fee per Billable Document</th></tr><tr><td>Tier 1 – Basic</td><td>10,000</td><td>10,100</td><td>₱7.00</td><td>₱8.75</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 2 – Standard</td><td>50,000</td><td>50,500</td><td>₱6.00</td><td>₱7.50</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 3 – Advanced</td><td>100,000</td><td>101,000</td><td>₱5.00</td><td>₱6.25</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 4 – Enterprise</td><td>999,999</td><td>1,009,998</td><td>₱4.00</td><td>₱5.00</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 5 – High Volume</td><td>No limit</td><td>No limit</td><td>₱3.00</td><td>None</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.2</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT and EPI shall assess the CLIENT’s plan together at onboarding, and the plan they agree is stated in the Client Details. It is the plan whose range contains the CLIENT’s average monthly number of invoices, receipts and other sales transactions over the twelve (12) months before the effective date, as shown by the usage history the CLIENT provides under Annex 2. The ranges are: Tier 1, up to 10,100; Tier 2, 10,101 to 50,500; Tier 3, 50,501 to 101,000; Tier 4, 101,001 to 1,009,998; and Tier 5, 1,009,999 and above. A CLIENT with no usage history, such as a new business, starts on Tier 1 and may move up under Section 4.5. It is the CLIENT’s responsibility to declare its usage truthfully and to choose the right plan. EPI’s assessment is not a guarantee that the plan fits, and the CLIENT has no claim against EPI because the plan it chose proved to be too high or too low. The CLIENT is billed on its actual use under Sections 4.3 and 4.4.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.3</span><span class=\"t\">Each Billable Document is charged at the fee of the CLIENT’s plan up to the Monthly Allowance plus a margin of one percent (1%). The margin is 10,100 Billable Documents for Tier 1, 50,500 for Tier 2, 101,000 for Tier 3 and 1,009,998 for Tier 4. Each Billable Document above that number in the same calendar month, and only those, is charged at the excess fee of the plan. The count starts again each calendar month. Tier 5 has no Monthly Allowance and no excess fee. For example, a Tier 2 CLIENT that issues 55,000 Billable Documents in a month pays 50,500 × ₱6.00 = ₱303,000, plus 4,500 × ₱7.50 = ₱33,750, a total of ₱336,750. Every client pays the same fees for the same plan.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.4</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall audit the CLIENT’s use each month from the records of the System. If the number of Billable Documents in a calendar month is below the range of the CLIENT’s plan in Section 4.2, EPI shall charge the Billable Documents of that month at the fee of the plan whose range contains the count, so that the CLIENT pays what a client on that plan would pay. The System still deducts the fee of the CLIENT’s own plan when each document is issued, and EPI shall bill the difference in the monthly statement under Section 4.9. For example, a Tier 2 CLIENT that issues 10,100 Billable Documents in a month has 10,100 × ₱6.00 = ₱60,600 deducted. The count falls in the Tier 1 range, so the charge is 10,100 × ₱7.00 = ₱70,700, and ₱10,100 is billed as the difference. Tier 1 has no lower range, so this Section does not apply to it.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.5</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT may move to a plan with a higher Monthly Allowance in any month, by written notice or through the System. The new plan applies from the first day of the next calendar month, without charge. At least thirty (30) days before each renewal, EPI shall send the CLIENT the results of its monthly audits for the term just ended, with the average monthly number of Billable Documents, and the PARTIES shall reassess the plan together under Section 4.2 using that average. The plan they agree applies from the renewal. If they do not agree, the current plan continues, and EPI’s monthly audit under Section 4.4 still bills the CLIENT for its actual use.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.6</span><span class=\"t\">The fee for each Billable Document is due when the document is issued. EPI shall collect it in real time, when the buyer pays through the System, by having the payment service provider deduct the fee, together with value-added tax, from the amount of the transaction and pay it to EPI. The provider settles the balance to the CLIENT’s bank or e-wallet account stated in the Client Details, in real time or on the next banking day (T+1), on its settlement schedule. The payment service provider is either the CLIENT’s own provider, which the CLIENT instructs in writing to make the deduction and keeps so instructed while this Agreement is in force, or a partner of EPI, as stated in the Client Details. The provider shall be authorized under Philippine law. EPI does not hold the CLIENT’s money. EPI shall show each transaction, the fee deducted and the amount settled in the System. If a document is not paid through the System, or the amount of the transaction is less than the fee, EPI shall bill the fee, or the part not deducted, in the monthly statement under Section 4.9. The fee is charged to the CLIENT only, and EPI shall not add it to the price the buyer pays. The charge of the payment service provider for processing a payment is between the provider and the party that pays it. EPI is not a party to it and is not responsible for it.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.7</span><span class=\"t\">Once an invoice is issued, EPI’s service for that Billable Document is complete. EPI shall not refund the fee for it, whether the CLIENT later voids the invoice or the buyer’s payment is refunded, reversed or charged back, except where the System caused the void, as Section 1.3 provides. The payment service provider acts under a separate agreement between the CLIENT and the provider. EPI is not a party to that agreement and is not responsible for the provider’s acts, delays, charges or failures, including late settlement to the CLIENT. If the CLIENT cancels or changes its instruction to the provider so that the fee cannot be deducted, EPI shall bill the fees in the monthly statement under Section 4.9, and the cancellation is a breach of this Agreement unless the CLIENT gives EPI a working replacement instruction within fifteen (15) days.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.8</span><span class=\"t\">Before the account is activated, the CLIENT shall pay EPI a refundable safety deposit for its plan (the “Safety Deposit”): Tier 1, ₱5,000; Tier 2, ₱11,000; Tier 3, ₱51,000; Tier 4, ₱101,000; and Tier 5, ₱1,010,000. The Safety Deposit is not a fee, a prepayment or a minimum charge, and the CLIENT may not use it in place of paying a bill. It secures the CLIENT’s payment of the amounts due under this Agreement, such as the difference EPI bills after its monthly audit under Section 4.4 and any fee that could not be deducted. If a bill is not paid by its due date, EPI may, after written notice, deduct the unpaid amount, with the interest and penalty, from the Safety Deposit, and the CLIENT shall restore the Safety Deposit to its full amount within fifteen (15) days. EPI holds the Safety Deposit without interest. If the CLIENT moves to a higher plan, it shall pay the difference in deposit before the new plan applies. If it moves to a lower plan, EPI shall refund the difference with the next statement. EPI shall refund the Safety Deposit when this Agreement ends, as Section 11.5 provides.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.9</span><span class=\"t\">After each month end, EPI shall send a statement showing the Billable Documents counted, the fees deducted in real time, the result of EPI’s monthly audit under Section 4.4, and any difference. A difference arises when a fee could not be fully deducted, when a deduction was wrong or was later cancelled, when the fee is adjusted under Section 4.4, or when the count in the System differs from the deductions. EPI shall bill the CLIENT for any amount short, due fifteen (15) days after the date of the bill, and shall refund any amount collected in excess. The CLIENT may see its Billable Document count and the fees deducted in the System at any time. The records of the System decide the count, unless the CLIENT shows a manifest error. A late payment carries interest of one percent (1%) a month on the unpaid amount, and a penalty of a further one percent (1%) a month, a total of two percent (2%) a month, from the due date until paid. If the CLIENT does not pay, EPI may take legal action to collect the amount due, and the CLIENT shall also pay reasonable attorney’s fees and costs of collection as the court allows.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.10</span><span class=\"t\">Only Billable Documents are charged, at the fees in Section 4.1 as adjusted under Section 4.4. These are all of EPI’s fees. There is no onboarding fee, no minimum monthly charge, and no charge for exporting or handing over the CLIENT’s records. There is no charge for branches, users or connections.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.11</span><span class=\"t\">The fees do not include value-added tax, which is added to each deduction and bill. EPI shall issue an E-Invoice for its fees as the law requires. EPI bills the fees to the CLIENT only and does not add them to the invoices the CLIENT issues to its buyers. If the law requires the CLIENT to withhold tax on the fees, the CLIENT may deduct it and shall give EPI the certificate of withholding within the time the law sets.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.12</span><span class=\"t\">If the CLIENT disputes a bill or a deduction, it shall tell EPI in writing within fifteen (15) days of the statement or bill date and pay the part not in dispute. The PARTIES shall settle the difference from the records of the System.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.13</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall give thirty (30) days’ written notice before any change in fees. A change applies only to Billable Documents issued after it takes effect. The CLIENT may end this Agreement without charge by written notice given before the change takes effect.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">4.14</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall not stop issuing, or restrict access to the CLIENT’s records, because a fee could not be deducted or fees are unpaid. EPI may recover unpaid fees by legal action, with the interest, penalty and costs in Section 4.9.</span></p>\n<h2>5.  OBLIGATIONS OF EPI</h2>\n<p class=\"l\">EPI shall:</p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(a)</span><span class=\"t\">provide and maintain access to the System in accordance with the Service Level Schedule;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(b)</span><span class=\"t\">generate, issue, preserve and verify E-Invoices, and transmit them as Article 2 requires;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(c)</span><span class=\"t\">apply technical and organizational measures to protect the System and the CLIENT Data, including encryption, access control with a second sign-in step, audit logging and backup;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(d)</span><span class=\"t\">tell the CLIENT of a material service interruption within thirty (30) minutes of declaring it, say what the CLIENT should do, and update the CLIENT every hour until it ends;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(e)</span><span class=\"t\">give technical support in accordance with the Service Level Schedule;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(f)</span><span class=\"t\">send the BIR a written report of a critical security incident within twenty-four (24) hours of discovering it, and notify the CLIENT of a breach affecting its data as Article 8 provides;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(g)</span><span class=\"t\">give the CLIENT, on request, a report showing the extent and cause of a transmission delay;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(h)</span><span class=\"t\">tell the CLIENT at least thirty (30) days before it changes the location where the CLIENT Data is hosted, adds or replaces a party listed in Schedule C, or makes a material change to the service, unless the BIR or an emergency requires shorter notice; and</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(i)</span><span class=\"t\">perform its obligations in accordance with this Agreement and with applicable laws, rules and regulations, including the issuances of the BIR.</span></p>\n<h2>6.  OBLIGATIONS OF THE CLIENT</h2>\n<p class=\"l\">The CLIENT shall:</p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(a)</span><span class=\"t\">give the information and cooperation reasonably needed to set up and use the System, including the documents listed in Annex 2;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(b)</span><span class=\"t\">name the authorized personnel who will work with EPI, and tell EPI in writing when the authority of any of them ends or changes;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(c)</span><span class=\"t\">pay the fees in accordance with Article 4;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(d)</span><span class=\"t\">use the System in accordance with this Agreement and applicable laws, rules and regulations;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(e)</span><span class=\"t\">ensure the accuracy of its invoices, books, returns and taxes. The CLIENT remains responsible for its own tax compliance;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(f)</span><span class=\"t\">keep its sign-in credentials secure and ensure that only authorized users use them;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(g)</span><span class=\"t\">tell EPI within ten (10) days of any change in its registered name, address, TIN registration, branches or authorized signatory;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(h)</span><span class=\"t\">follow the contingency procedure when the System or the BIR EIS is unavailable, which is to issue manual invoices as the law allows and record them in the System afterwards;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(i)</span><span class=\"t\">confirm, and keep confirmed, that it legally exists and is registered with the BIR under the TIN stated in the Client Details, and that its signatory has authority to sign this Agreement and to give the authorizations in Annex 1; and</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">(j)</span><span class=\"t\">ensure that the information and documents it gives EPI are true and complete, and update them when they change.</span></p>\n<h2>7.  SERVICE LEVELS</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">7.1</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall attain and provide the service levels in the Service Level Schedule (Schedule B) and the minimum service-level requirements the BIR prescribes, including system availability, recovery time and recovery point objectives, transmission-success rates, acknowledgment processing times, incident response times and support response times. Where the BIR prescribes a higher level, the BIR’s level applies. Where the BIR has not yet prescribed a level, the figure in Schedule B is EPI’s target.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">7.2</span><span class=\"t\">EPI measures availability by probes every minute from outside the System and from its interruption log, the same record that drives the Online and Offline indicator shown to the CLIENT. EPI shall give the CLIENT a monthly service report, by the fifth (5th) working day of the next month, showing availability, the number of invoices sent and acknowledged, delays and their causes, and incidents.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">7.3</span><span class=\"t\">These do not count against EPI’s availability or transmission-success levels: an interruption of the BIR EIS; an interruption of a platform or API the CLIENT uses; a failure of the CLIENT’s own equipment or internet; an invoice rejected because of the CLIENT’s data; planned maintenance under Schedule B; emergency work to fix a serious weakness, with notice as early as possible; and a directive of the BIR. The loss of the whole cloud region does count as an interruption of EPI’s service.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">7.4</span><span class=\"t\">After a month in which availability is below the target, EPI shall give the CLIENT a written explanation within five (5) working days, stating the cause and the corrective actions. After three (3) months in a row below the target, or any month below ninety-nine percent (99%), EPI’s technical focal person and management shall hold a recorded review.</span></p>\n<h2>8.  CLIENT DATA, PRIVACY AND CONFIDENTIALITY</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.1</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT owns the CLIENT Data. Invoice and sales data sent to or held for the BIR is an official tax-administration record. EPI has no ownership, lien or intellectual-property claim over any of it.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.2</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall use the CLIENT Data only to provide invoicing, reporting, storage, support, security, reconciliation and legal compliance. EPI shall not sell, monetize, profile or combine the CLIENT Data, or use it for advertising, credit scoring, competitive intelligence or any unrelated purpose.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.3</span><span class=\"t\">No affiliate, hosting provider, cloud provider, subcontractor or foreign processor may access or process the CLIENT Data unless it is disclosed to and approved by the BIR and bound in writing to obligations at least equal to EPI’s. The parties EPI uses at the date of this Agreement are listed in the Data Schedule (Schedule C).</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.4</span><span class=\"t\">The BIR may access the CLIENT’s invoice data, metadata, audit logs, acknowledgments and rejections as the law allows.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.5</span><span class=\"t\">Each PARTY shall comply with the Data Privacy Act of 2012 (Republic Act No. 10173) and its rules. The CLIENT is the personal information controller of the personal data of its buyers and shall have a lawful basis to give it to EPI. EPI processes that data only on the CLIENT’s instructions as its personal information processor. EPI shall notify the CLIENT of a personal data breach affecting the CLIENT Data without undue delay and within seventy-two (72) hours of confirming it.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.6</span><span class=\"t\">Each PARTY shall keep confidential the non-public information it receives from the other under this Agreement and shall protect it with appropriate safeguards. This does not apply to information the law or the BIR requires it to disclose.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">8.7</span><span class=\"t\">EPI is the personal information controller of the personal data of the CLIENT’s users, such as their names, email addresses and sign-in records, and shall use it only to provide and secure the service. EPI shall help the CLIENT respond to requests of data subjects and to inquiries of the National Privacy Commission about the CLIENT Data. EPI’s data protection officer is named in Schedule C.</span></p>\n<h2>9.  RECORDS, EXIT AND PORTABILITY</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">9.1</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall keep the CLIENT’s E-Invoices, sales data, metadata, acknowledgments, error records and audit trails for the period the Tax Code requires and afterwards, until the BIR confirms in writing that the data has been completely and successfully migrated to the BIR and allows EPI to stop storing it. EPI shall not delete records by reason of age alone.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">9.2</span><span class=\"t\">EPI’s duties of storage, access, preservation, confidentiality, audit support and migration continue after this Agreement ends, and continue in case of insolvency, cessation of business, merger or change in control.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">9.3</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall not withhold, disable, delete, degrade or condition access to the CLIENT Data because of a dispute, unpaid fees, termination of this Agreement or a change of provider.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">9.4</span><span class=\"t\">At the CLIENT’s request, and in any case when this Agreement ends, EPI shall deliver within five (5) working days an export package of the CLIENT Data, including documents, metadata, logs and audit trails, in JSON, CSV and PDF with a field dictionary and SHA-256 checksums. EPI shall deliver it to the CLIENT, the BIR or another accredited provider, as the CLIENT directs, by secure link or direct transfer, at no charge.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">9.5</span><span class=\"t\">EPI shall support any legal hold or preservation order that applies to the CLIENT’s records.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">9.6</span><span class=\"t\">After the BIR confirms migration and allows EPI to stop storing the records, EPI shall delete them and give the CLIENT a certificate of deletion.</span></p>\n<h2>10.  ACCOUNTABILITY</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">10.1</span><span class=\"t\">EPI is accountable to the BIR and to the CLIENT for compliance with the terms of its accreditation and with the BIR issuances that govern it.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">10.2</span><span class=\"t\">EPI is responsible for the CLIENT’s direct losses and reasonable remediation costs that result from EPI’s fraud, willful misconduct, gross negligence or material breach; from unauthorized access to, disclosure, alteration or deletion of the CLIENT Data; from a transmission failure caused by EPI’s systems or subcontractors; and from a failure of storage, backup or portability. This is subject to notice to EPI, an opportunity to be heard, and proof that the breach caused the loss.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">10.3</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT is responsible for the accuracy of the data it enters and for its own tax compliance.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">10.4</span><span class=\"t\">No term of this Agreement limits or waives a responsibility of EPI under the BIR issuances or the law. A term that does so has no effect to that extent.</span></p>\n<h2>11.  TERM AND TERMINATION</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.1</span><span class=\"t\">This Agreement takes effect when both PARTIES have signed it and EPI has received the signed copy. It runs for one (1) year and renews for successive periods of one (1) year unless either PARTY gives written notice of non-renewal at least sixty (60) days before the end of the period.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.2</span><span class=\"t\">The CLIENT may end this Agreement at any time on thirty (30) days’ written notice. EPI may end it without cause only on ninety (90) days’ written notice.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.3</span><span class=\"t\">Either PARTY may end this Agreement by written notice if the other commits a material breach and does not cure it within thirty (30) days of notice, or becomes insolvent.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.4</span><span class=\"t\">EPI may stop new invoicing only on a directive of the BIR, if the BIR suspends or revokes the CLIENT’s PTI Electronic Invoice registration, after a security incident that requires it, under Section 2.7, or at the CLIENT’s request.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.5</span><span class=\"t\">When this Agreement ends, new issuing stops on the effective date, the CLIENT pays the fees due up to that date, EPI refunds the Safety Deposit, after setting off any amount due, within thirty (30) days after the final statement, and EPI delivers the export package under Article 9. Article 4, as to fees due, and Articles 8, 9, 10 and 12 continue.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.6</span><span class=\"t\">If EPI’s accreditation is suspended, restricted or revoked, or the BIR directs an exit, migration or continuity measure, EPI shall carry it out, keep the CLIENT’s records as Article 9 requires, deliver the export package under Section 9.4 at no charge, and help the CLIENT move to another accredited provider. The CLIENT may end this Agreement at once, without charge, by written notice.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">11.7</span><span class=\"t\">If the CLIENT ceases business or its BIR registration is cancelled, EPI shall keep the CLIENT’s records as Article 9 requires and follow the BIR’s instructions on them. The fees due up to that date remain payable.</span></p>\n<h2>12.  GENERAL PROVISIONS</h2>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.1</span><span class=\"t\">Notices. Notices shall be in writing and sent to the address or email in the Client Details, or to a new address the other PARTY has been told in writing.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.2</span><span class=\"t\">Amendments. An amendment of this Agreement shall be in writing and signed by the authorized representatives of both PARTIES. Fee changes follow Section 4.13. A change that the law or a BIR directive requires takes effect as the law or directive requires, on notice to the CLIENT.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.3</span><span class=\"t\">Assignment. Neither PARTY may assign this Agreement without the written consent of the other. EPI may transfer it to another BIR-accredited provider the BIR approves. EPI shall tell the CLIENT in writing within thirty (30) days of a change in its control.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.4</span><span class=\"t\">Events beyond control. Neither PARTY is liable for a failure caused by an event beyond its reasonable control, if it acts promptly to limit the effect. This does not excuse payment of fees due, or EPI’s duties of retention and recovery.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.5</span><span class=\"t\">Settlement of disputes. The PARTIES shall first try to settle a dispute by good-faith consultation. If it is not settled within thirty (30) days, either PARTY may bring it before the proper courts of Quezon City, to the exclusion of other venues.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.6</span><span class=\"t\">Governing law. Philippine law governs this Agreement.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.7</span><span class=\"t\">Severability. If a term is found invalid or unenforceable, the rest of this Agreement remains in force.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.8</span><span class=\"t\">Entire agreement. This Agreement, with its Schedules and Annexes, is the whole agreement of the PARTIES on the provision and use of the System. It replaces earlier discussions and understandings on the same subject, including the Terms of Use the CLIENT accepted when applying, to the extent permitted by law. If a Schedule or Annex conflicts with the Articles, the Articles prevail.</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl j\"><span class=\"n\">12.9</span><span class=\"t\">Signing. This Agreement is signed in ink by the authorized representatives of both PARTIES and notarized. The CLIENT shall upload the signed and notarized copy to the System, and shall give EPI the original copy on request.</span></p>\n<p class=\"j\">IN WITNESS WHEREOF, the PARTIES have signed this Agreement on the date and at the place first written above.</p>\n<table class=\"sig\"><tr><td>{{name}}<br>CLIENT<br>By: ______________________________<br>Name: {{signatory}}<br>Position: {{position}}</td><td>EASTLAND PRINTINK, INC.<br>EPI<br>By: ______________________________<br>Name: ____________________________<br>Position: ____________________________</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"l\"><b>SIGNED IN THE PRESENCE OF:</b></p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Witness<br><br>By: ______________________________<br>Name: ____________________________</th><th>Witness<br><br>By: ______________________________<br>Name: ____________________________</th></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<div class=\"pb\"></div>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>ACKNOWLEDGMENT</b></p>\n<p class=\"l\">REPUBLIC OF THE PHILIPPINES&emsp;&emsp;)<br>____________________________&emsp;)  S.S.</p>\n<p class=\"j\">BEFORE ME, a Notary Public for and in ______________________, this ____ day of ______________ 20____, personally appeared:</p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Name</th><th>Competent Evidence of Identity</th><th>Date and Place Issued</th></tr><tr><td>{{signatory}}</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"j\">known to me and to me known to be the same persons who signed the foregoing Client Service Agreement, consisting of ____ pages, including the pages on which the Schedules, Annexes and this Acknowledgment are written, and each page signed by the PARTIES and their witnesses. They acknowledged to me that the same is their free and voluntary act and deed and that of the entities they respectively represent.</p>\n<p class=\"j\">WITNESS MY HAND AND NOTARIAL SEAL on the date and at the place first written above.</p>\n<p class=\"l\">Notary Public</p>\n<p class=\"l\">Doc. No. _____;<br>Page No. _____;<br>Book No. _____;<br>Series of _____.</p>\n<div class=\"pb\"></div>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>SCHEDULE A</b></p>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>CLIENT DETAILS</b></p>\n<p class=\"j\">The System fills in the CLIENT’s details when it prepares this Agreement. The CLIENT checks them before signing.</p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>Client name</td><td>{{name}}</td></tr><tr><td>Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN)</td><td>{{tin}}</td></tr><tr><td>Type of taxpayer</td><td>{{type}}</td></tr><tr><td>Registered address</td><td>{{address}}</td></tr><tr><td>Authorized signatory (name and position)</td><td>{{signatory}}, {{position}}</td></tr><tr><td>Contact person, email and mobile number</td><td>{{contact}}</td></tr><tr><td>Address for notices</td><td>{{notice_address}}</td></tr><tr><td>Effective date</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Term</td><td>One (1) year, renewing yearly (Section 11.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Plan (Section 4.2)</td><td>{{plan}}</td></tr><tr><td>Average monthly invoices, receipts and transactions, past 12 months (Annex 2), or the estimate of a new business</td><td>{{usage}}</td></tr><tr><td>Safety Deposit paid (Section 4.8)</td><td>{{deposit}}</td></tr><tr><td>Payment service provider (Section 4.6)</td><td>{{provider}}</td></tr><tr><td>Bank or e-wallet account for settlement (Section 4.6)</td><td>{{settlement}}</td></tr><tr><td>EPI accreditation reference</td><td>Certificate No. ____________  dated ____________</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"l\"><b>Branches covered by this Agreement</b></p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Branch code</th><th>Branch name</th><th>Registered address</th></tr>{{branch_rows}}</table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<div class=\"pb\"></div>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>SCHEDULE B</b></p>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>SERVICE LEVEL SCHEDULE</b></p>\n<p class=\"j\">The areas below follow those the BIR requires EPI to meet. Where the BIR has not prescribed a figure, the figure here is EPI’s target. Where the BIR prescribes a figure, the BIR’s figure applies.</p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Area</th><th>Commitment</th></tr><tr><td>1. System availability</td><td>99.9% each calendar month for sign-in and issuing, about 43 minutes of interruption at most, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. Counts interruptions of EPI’s own service only (Section 7.3)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Recovery time (RTO)</td><td>4 hours after a server, database or data center failure. 24 hours if the whole cloud region is lost</td></tr><tr><td>3. Recovery point (RPO)</td><td>15 minutes of data, at most</td></tr><tr><td>4. Transmission-success rate</td><td>At least 99.5% of invoices are acknowledged by the BIR on the first or a later automatic attempt. At least 99% are acknowledged within 1 hour of issue when the BIR EIS is available. No invoice is sent later than 3 calendar days after the transaction. Invoices held back by a BIR EIS interruption, or rejected for the CLIENT’s data, are counted and shown separately. Queued invoices are resent within 3 calendar days after the connection is restored, without loss, alteration, corruption or duplication</td></tr><tr><td>5. Acknowledgment processing time</td><td>The BIR’s acknowledgment or rejection, with the reason, is stored on the invoice and shown to the CLIENT within 1 minute of EPI receiving it</td></tr><tr><td>6. Incident response time</td><td>As in the rating table below. The CLIENT is told within 30 minutes of EPI declaring a material interruption. A written report goes to the BIR within 24 hours of discovering a critical incident</td></tr><tr><td>7. Support response time</td><td>As in the rating table below. Support hours Monday to Friday, 8:00 to 18:00 Asia/Manila. Requests are made through Report a problem in the System, which gives a reference number, or by email. Critical incidents may also be reported by phone</td></tr><tr><td>8. Data retrieval</td><td>The CLIENT can view and export its own records in the System at any time. The full export package is delivered within 5 working days of the request or of the end of the Agreement. For requests of the BIR: a summary report at once and in any case within 1 working day, and a data extract within 3 working days, or the period the BIR prescribes</td></tr><tr><td>Buyer copies</td><td>A copy of each invoice is sent to the buyer within 5 minutes of issue, and retried automatically if it fails</td></tr><tr><td>Interruptions</td><td>While the BIR EIS or an API is down, issuing continues and invoices are held and sent when it returns. While EPI’s service is down, sales are queued and issued in order when it returns. Invoices issued manually are recorded and linked to their electronic copies. On request, EPI gives a report showing the delay and its cause</td></tr><tr><td>Daily reconciliation</td><td>Each day, after the day’s invoices are recorded, EPI compares its records with the BIR’s, retries what was not acknowledged, and shows each invoice’s status to the CLIENT</td></tr><tr><td>Planned maintenance</td><td>One window a month, Sunday 00:00 to 04:00 Asia/Manila, with 7 days’ notice. Sales entered in the window are queued and issued afterwards. Other releases cause no downtime</td></tr><tr><td>After a miss</td><td>As Section 7.4</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"l\"><b>Rating table</b></p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Rating</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Response</th><th>Update</th><th>Fix or workaround</th></tr><tr><td>Critical</td><td>Service down, data at risk, or transmission stopped</td><td>15 minutes, all hours</td><td>Every hour</td><td>Workaround within 4 hours</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>One function is unusable, or one client is blocked</td><td>2 business hours</td><td>Every 4 hours</td><td>1 business day</td></tr><tr><td>Normal</td><td>A fault with a workaround, or a question</td><td>1 business day</td><td>On request</td><td>5 business days</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<div class=\"pb\"></div>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>SCHEDULE C</b></p>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>DATA SCHEDULE: PARTIES THAT HANDLE THE CLIENT DATA</b></p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Party</th><th>Purpose</th><th>What it handles</th></tr><tr><td>Microsoft (Azure)</td><td>Hosting, database, document storage and monitoring</td><td>All CLIENT Data, stored in Azure Southeast Asia (Singapore)</td></tr><tr><td>EPI, Valenzuela City</td><td>Backup copy of the database and documents</td><td>Encrypted backups of the CLIENT Data. Serves no clients</td></tr><tr><td>Brevo</td><td>Email delivery of invoice copies to buyers and of notices to the CLIENT</td><td>Recipient email address and the copy being sent</td></tr><tr><td>The payment service provider named in the Client Details (the CLIENT’s own, or EPI’s partner), under its own agreement with the CLIENT</td><td>Collecting the buyer’s payment, deducting EPI’s fee and settling the balance to the CLIENT</td><td>Buyer payment details handled by the provider, the amount, and the CLIENT’s settlement account</td></tr><tr><td>A Philippine mobile carrier (such as Globe or Smart)</td><td>Text message delivery when email fails, and sign-in codes</td><td>Recipient mobile number and the message</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"l\">Each party is bound to obligations at least equal to EPI’s. EPI shall tell the CLIENT and the BIR before adding or replacing a party.</p>\n<p class=\"l\">EPI data protection officer:  Name ______________________   Email ______________________</p>\n<div class=\"pb\"></div>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>ANNEX 1</b></p>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>CLIENT AUTHORIZATION</b></p>\n<p class=\"l\">To:  Eastland Printink, Inc. (EPI)<br>92 R. Jacinto St., Canumay West, Valenzuela City 1443</p>\n<p class=\"l\">We, {{name}}, with TIN {{tin}}, represented by {{signatory}}, in the capacity of {{position}}, authorize EPI, for as long as our Client Service Agreement with EPI is in force, to:</p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span><span class=\"t\">facilitate the application for, issuance of, or updating of our PTI Electronic Invoice through the procedure the BIR prescribes;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span><span class=\"t\">manage the API integrations, certifications and pre-production testing the BIR requires;</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span><span class=\"t\">transmit our E-Invoices and sales data to the BIR EIS; and</span></p>\n<p class=\"cl2 j\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span><span class=\"t\">arrange the notarization of our Client Service Agreement, by booking the notary, delivering the documents and collecting the notarized copies.</span></p>\n<p class=\"j\">We remain the holder of our PTI Electronic Invoice registration. EPI acquires no interest in or control over it. EPI is not authorized to sign any document for us. This authorization may be withdrawn by written notice to EPI.</p>\n<p class=\"j\">If the signatory is not the owner or the principal officer, attach the secretary’s certificate, board resolution or special power of attorney that authorizes the signatory.</p>\n<p class=\"l\">Signed on ____________ 20____, at ____________________.</p>\n<p class=\"l\">______________________________<br>Name and position of authorized signatory</p>\n<div class=\"pb\"></div>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>ANNEX 2</b></p>\n<p class=\"c\"><b>DOCUMENTS THE CLIENT PROVIDES</b></p>\n<p class=\"j\">EPI checks that the CLIENT legally exists and that its signatory may act for it before accepting the CLIENT. The CLIENT uploads these documents to the System.</p>\n<table class=\"grid\"><tr><th>Document</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>BIR Certificate of Registration, showing the TIN, and the certificate of each branch to be covered</td><td>Confirms the CLIENT’s registration with the BIR and its branches</td></tr><tr><td>SEC Certificate of Incorporation and Articles of Incorporation, or DTI Certificate of Business Name Registration, or CDA Certificate of Registration, as applicable</td><td>Confirms legal existence with the registering authority</td></tr><tr><td>Secretary’s certificate or board resolution, or special power of attorney, naming the signatory</td><td>Shows that the signatory may sign this Agreement, give EPI authorizations and attest to the CLIENT’s tax profile</td></tr><tr><td>Valid government-issued identification of the signatory</td><td>Confirms the signatory’s identity. The same identification is shown to the notary</td></tr><tr><td>Usage history: the number of invoices, receipts and other sales transactions issued in each of the past twelve (12) months, from the CLIENT’s system, books or sales reports. A new business states in writing that it has none</td><td>Shows the average monthly usage, so that EPI can confirm the plan under Section 4.2</td></tr><tr><td>Signed and notarized copy of this Agreement, with Annex 1</td><td>Proof that the CLIENT has agreed. Uploaded after notarization</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"sp\">&nbsp;</p>"};
